course: dictionary
lesson: 12740
title: A drover and the long road
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A drover is a person who moves cattle or sheep from one place to another, usually on foot. You can work this out from the words around it. «The drover walked two hundred cattle to the market town.» Cattle are cows, and they are being taken to a town, so a drover must be the person who takes them there.
explain: Numbers in the text give more clues. «Each day the drover walked twenty miles, so the trip took ten days.» Twenty miles a day for ten days makes two hundred miles. Reading and maths work together here, and the numbers help you check your meaning.
explain: Always test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Put "a person who moves animals on foot" where drover is, and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have understood the word.
fact: A drover moves cattle or sheep from one place to another, usually on foot.
fact: Drovers often travel long distances, so a journey can take many days.
fact: Drovers were common long ago, and still work in places like Australia.
fact: To find the number of days on a journey, divide the total distance by the miles covered each day.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word, and they often give clues to its meaning.
exercise: «The drover walked two hundred cattle to the market town.» What is a drover? | *A person who moves cattle or sheep on foot | A large cow that lives in the hills | A shop worker in a market town
exercise: «Each day the drover walked twenty miles. The trip took ten days.» How far did the drover walk in total? | 30 miles | 10 miles | 2 miles | *200 miles
exercise: A drover walks 15 miles each day. The journey is 150 miles. How many days will the trip take? | 135 days | 15 days | 150 days | *10 days
test: «Drovers were common long ago, and still work in places like Australia.» Where do drovers still work today, according to this sentence? | In shops | Only in old stories | *In places like Australia | On the moon
test: What does the word drover mean? | A large sheep with thick wool | A wooden fence around a field | A person who writes books | *A person who moves cattle or sheep from place to place on foot
exam: Ella reads «The drover and his herd crossed the hills for three weeks.» What does drover mean in this sentence? | *A person who moves a herd of animals on foot | A teacher of young animals | A traveller who sells cloth | A farm worker who never leaves home
